  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nWe adopt a two-stage approach: first, construct a highly structured candidate based on a periodic expansion of a base function optimized via constrained optimization, and second, perform adaptive resolution refinement. This differs from the previous library-based optimization by:\n- Explicit mathematical basis (convolution of a base function with its shifts)\n- Hierarchical formation of candidates from explicit mathematical transformations\n- Focus on signal processing features (oscillatory patterns) to minimize maximum autocorrelation\nThe key innovation is viewing the overlap operator as a convolution, allowing direct control over the autocorrelation structure. This captures global shape information rather than local adjustments. The bottleneck addressed is the tendency of local search to miss functional forms that are globally optimal for shift-invariance.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nCandidates generated by:\n  h(x) = A(size, freq, phas) = sinc2( (x - phas) * freq ) \n  Constrained optimization: Apply Tikhonov regularization for stable inversion\n  Integral normalization: Use FFT-based global integral approximation\n  Mathematical continuity: Piecewise smooth versions of the base functions\nObjective transformation: Directly minimize the maximum autocorrelation\nConstraint handling: \n  Integral=1 enforced via dual variable projector (Lagrange multipliers)\n  Bounds handled by exact penalty functions\nWe provide a instance-based candidate space defined through the latent parameters size, freq, and phase.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nStartup:\n  - Parent validation: For initial_conditions, apply Fourier projection to ensure periodic and smooth applicability\n  - Period transformation: Build periodic structure from base Fourier components scaled by optimal length\n  - Main cycle:\n    Iteration 1: Constrained optimization via gradient projection (Nelder-Mead wrapped in limits)\n    Iteration 2: Functional validation (check autocorrelation, integral)\n    Iteration 3: Progressive resolution (retretion scales)\n  - Global search: Basin hopping using frequency-space mutations\n\nParameter Setup:\n  size_base \u2208 [4, 48] (fundamental wavelength)\n  freq_factor \u2208 (0.25, 4.0)\n  phase_offset \u2208 [0, \u03c4)\n  dx = 2.0 / n_base_points (starting)\n\nConstraint projection:\n  \u03c4_c(t) = \u03c4_c(t-1) if no improvement else \u03c4_c(t-1) * 0.98 (cooling schedule for fixed-parameter optimization)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n  {Initial}: Base period determined by parent distribution\n  {Safety}: Implement fault tolerance for ill-conditioned optimizations\n  {Resolution}: Automatically increase sample count modestly if nudge occurs\n  {Caching}: Save best candidates per optimization run\n\nOptimization Path:\n  - Objective: Minimize max(autocorrelation components)\n  - Lexicographical stages: First integral, then autocorrelation\n  - Hybrid ga: We say to use the lattice strength gene to make it work.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n  Fixed phases: 8 * 4 * 4 = 128 candidates parallelized across 4 cores\n  Budget: 1100s -> 280 iterations per core\n  Body check: Every 1000/candidate - perform integral and autocorrelation verification\n  Abort signals: \n    - Optimization stuck after 2000 iterations\n    - No improvement for 200 minutes\n  Fallback: Maintain parent if still valid from initial_conditions\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n  - Integral check: FFT integration for validation\n  - Value range: 0 to 1 enforced\n  - Objective: Resample through formula reference implementation for C\n  - Format consistency: List(h), float(C5), n_points\n  - Abandon condition: Candidate shows 1e-2 worse than best known and stuck\n\nStructure diagrams:\n  L1: Parse transform parameters from parent\n  L2: Constrained optimization -> only update if gradient check passes\n  L3: Verify using independent integration\n  L4: Heap insertion of new candidates\n\nExample formula:\n  h[x] = T(real part (Fourier basis period * phase))\n\nTechnologically required:\n  EXPLICIT candidate family through fft.py (function library)\n  Strict return value monitoring\n  Fallback to meta-heuristics if gradient estimation fails\n\nImplementation tokens:\n  - Use 'sicihocoder' tag for gradient-stable operations\n  - Implement hardware Lewis memory management\n\nSolution checks:\n  - Best record should be below 0.38087\n  - Valid candidate must be properly normalized\n  - Algorithm must be a true mathematical reformulation",
